Using Node.js for local development, especially for server-side applications, has several advantages over directly using JavaScript engines like V8. Here's why Node.js is often preferred:

1. Server-Side Environment
Purpose: Node.js is designed specifically for server-side applications, whereas V8 is primarily a JavaScript engine that executes JavaScript code. Node.js provides a runtime environment that includes all the necessary libraries and APIs for server-side development.
Functionality: With Node.js, you have access to modules and features tailored for building networked applications (e.g., HTTP, file system, streams, etc.), which are not available when using V8 alone.
2. Event-Driven Architecture
Asynchronous I/O: Node.js utilizes an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model. This makes it highly efficient and suitable for I/O-heavy applications, such as web servers. While V8 can execute JavaScript, it doesn’t provide the event-driven architecture or the event loop that Node.js does.
Concurrency: Node.js allows you to handle many connections simultaneously without creating a new thread for each request, which can be a more resource-efficient approach compared to traditional server models.
3. Built-In Libraries and Modules
Rich Ecosystem: Node.js comes with a vast number of built-in libraries and a package manager (npm) that allows developers to easily install and manage third-party libraries. This is not available with V8 alone.
Quick Development: The availability of these libraries and modules facilitates rapid application development, as developers can leverage existing tools rather than building everything from scratch.
4. Cross-Platform Compatibility
Run Anywhere: Node.js applications can run on various platforms (Windows, macOS, Linux) without requiring modification. This is particularly useful for developers who want to ensure that their applications are portable across different environments.
Development Consistency: When using Node.js, you can maintain a consistent development experience across local, staging, and production environments.
5. Community and Support
Active Community: Node.js has a large and active community, which means that developers can find support, tutorials, and resources easily. This community contributes to the continuous improvement of the Node.js ecosystem.
Frameworks: There are many popular frameworks built on top of Node.js (like Express.js) that simplify the development of web applications and APIs.
6. Real-Time Capabilities
WebSocket Support: Node.js natively supports WebSockets, making it easier to build real-time applications such as chat apps, gaming servers, and live notifications.
7. Development Tools
Debugging and Tooling: Node.js provides development tools that simplify debugging and monitoring applications, including features like logging, performance profiling, and more.
